title: 'pallet-revive: cold/hot pricing for calls, transfers, code loads and contract creation'
doc:
- audience: [Runtime Dev, Runtime User]
  description: |-
    The cold/hot pricing introduced in #12104 now also covers the state items accessed by the call
    opcodes (EVM `CALL`, `STATICCALL`, `DELEGATECALL` and the PVM call and delegate-call host
    functions), a call's value transfer, code loads and contract creation, following EIP-2929. An
    item is hot if it was accessed earlier in the same transaction. Each operation is charged the
    cold benchmark if any of the items it accesses is cold, and the hot benchmark if all are hot.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `WeightInfo` has 14 new required methods: `code_load`, `chained_delegation_check`,
    `seal_call_hot`, `seal_call_transfer_hot`, `seal_delegate_call_hot`,
    `call_with_pvm_code_per_byte_hot`, `call_with_evm_code_per_byte_hot`,
    `access_list_touch_hot_upgrade`, `access_list_touch_hot_address_upgrade`,
    `access_list_touch_cold_address_empty`, `access_list_touch_cold_address_full`,
    `access_list_touch_hot_address_nearly_empty`, `access_list_touch_hot_address_full` and
    `access_list_rollback_address_amortization`.
    Some existing benchmarks also measure different work now, so runtimes with their own
    `pallet_revive` weights must regenerate them:
    - `call`, `eth_call`, `seal_call`, `seal_delegate_call`, `instantiate` and `seal_instantiate`
      no longer measure the code reads, which `code_load` charges separately. Their weights drop,
      but a cold call or instantiation now also pays `code_load`.
    - `seal_instantiate` and `evm_instantiate` also measure a read of the new address's
      `OriginalAccount` entry.
    - The access-list touch benchmarks, `access_list_touch_cold_empty`,
      `access_list_touch_cold_full`, `access_list_touch_hot_full` and the new ones, take the
      number of touches `n` and measure `n` touches per run. `access_list_touch_hot_single_element`
      is renamed to `access_list_touch_hot_nearly_empty` and touches `n` entries of a nearly
      empty list.
      `access_list_touch_hot_full` no longer measures an upgrade, which
      `access_list_touch_hot_upgrade` now does.
    - Since the access list grew from 2,048 to 4,096 entries, the benchmarks that fill it now go
      up to 4,096.

    Code outside the pallet that uses `pallet_revive::precompiles`:
    - `Ext` no longer has `touch_storage_access` and `peek_storage_access`, and
      `StorageAccessKind::Persistent` no longer holds a plain `Warmth`, so `StorageAccessKind::new`
      no longer takes one either. Code that charged a storage access should charge the cold price
      with the new `StorageAccessKind::new_persistent_cold`, or use `StorageAccessKind::Transient` for
      transient storage, for example:
      ```rust
      env.frame_meter_mut().charge_weight_token(RuntimeCosts::GetStorage {
          len,
          kind: StorageAccessKind::new_persistent_cold(),
      })?;
      ```
    - `StorageOp` and `Warmth` are no longer re-exported.
    - In `RuntimeCosts`, `DelegateCallBase` is removed, `CallBase` takes the warmth of the call's
      entries as a new parameter, `CallTransferSurcharge` takes the transfer's warmth in place of
      `dust_transfer`, and `ChainedDelegationCheck` and `AccessListRegistration` are new. Code that
      charged `CallBase`, `DelegateCallBase` or `CallTransferSurcharge` can no longer build these
      variants; it should charge the cold price from `WeightInfo` instead, for example
      `env.charge(T::WeightInfo::seal_call(transfer, dust, 0))`, or `seal_delegate_call()` for a
      delegate call.

    `ContractBlob::store_code` now returns `Result<Option<BalanceOf<T>>, DispatchError>`. On
    success it holds the deposit collected for newly stored code, or `None` when the code was
    already stored.

    `ExecConfig` has a new field, `first_frame_reads_paid`, which `with_first_frame_reads_paid`
    sets. It adds the called contract's entries to the access list, so a call back into the
    contract is charged hot. Set it only when the weight already covers these reads, as the
    weights of the `call` and `eth_call` dispatchables do. A dry run must set it exactly when the
    dispatch it estimates does, or its estimate can be too low; the runtime APIs' dry runs of
    `call` and `eth_call` set it. Code that calls `bare_call` directly should leave it unset,
    unless its weight already covers these reads too. Code that builds an `ExecConfig` by listing
    all its fields, instead of calling a constructor such as `ExecConfig::new_substrate_tx()`,
    must add `first_frame_reads_paid`.

    `pallet_revive::runtime_api` has a new public function, `dry_run_call`. It dry runs the `call`
    dispatchable the same way the `call` runtime API does.

    The access list holds up to 4,096 entries per transaction (was 2,048). The memory budgeted
    for its worst case, which `integrity_test` counts against `RuntimeMemory`, grows from 1.5 MiB
    to 4 MiB, so a runtime close to its memory limit can now fail that check.

    No storage migration is needed.
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    A call or delegate call to an EIP-7702 delegated account whose delegation target is not a
    deployed contract now also pays for reading the target's account info, which tells whether
    the target is itself delegated.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
